ast one ticker\")\n    w = 1.0 / len(tickers)\n    return (w,) * len(tickers)\n```\n\nMomentumStrategy:\n\n```python\nclass MomentumStrategy(Strategy):\n    \"\"\"Cross-sectional momentum: rotate top-N monthly on last-trading-day\n    scheduled days; weights sum to 1.0; non-scheduled days carry over.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, cfg: Settings) -> None:\n        self.cfg = cfg\n\n    def generate_target_weights(\n        self,\n        prices: pd.DataFrame,\n        features: pd.DataFrame,\n        as_of: date,\n    ) -> PositionSet:\n        if features.empty:\n            raise ValueError(\"generate_target_weights: empty features frame\")\n        idx = features.index\n        if not isinstance(idx, pd.DatetimeIndex):\n            raise ValueError(\"generate_target_weights: features must be indexed by date\")\n        idx = idx.sort_values()\n        horizon = pd.Timestamp(as_of)\n        if idx[-1] > horizon:\n            raise ValueError(f\"as_of {as_of} is before the last feature date {idx[-1]:%Y-%m-%d}\")\n```\n\nHmm — I don't want to raise in the general case; better to filter up to horizon. But if horizon is before the first date → empty PositionSet. I'll just filter:\n\n```python\n        months = idx.to_period(\"M\")\n        last_per_month = (\n            idx.to_frame(\"d\").assign(m=months).groupby(\"m\")[\"d\"].max()\n        )\n        sched = set(last_per_month)\n        tickers = [str(t) for t in features.columns]\n\n        rows: dict[pd.Timestamp, pd.Series] = {}\n        current: pd.Series | None = None\n        for d in idx:\n            if d > horizon:\n                break\n            if d in sched:\n                top = select_top_n(features.loc[d], self.cfg.top_n)\n                if top:\n                    current = (\n                        pd.Series(dict(zip(top, equal_weights(top))))\n                        .reindex(tickers, fill_value=0.0)\n                    )\n            if current is None:\n                continue\n            rows[d] = current\n        if not rows:\n            raise ValueError(\"generate_target_weights: no scheduled date on or before as_of\")\n        weights = pd.DataFrame.from_dict(rows, orient=\"index\").T\n        return PositionSet(\n            dates=[d.date() for d in sorted(rows)],\n            weights=weights,\n        )\n```\n\nWait `features.loc[d]` — d is a Timestamp from the DatetimeIndex ✓. But `d in sched` — sched contains Timestamps (the max of \"d\" column, which is datetime64 → Timestamp). The loop variable d from `for d in idx` is a Timestamp ✓.\n\n`last_per_month` — groupby(\"m\") on a PeriodIndex column: `.groupby(\"m\")[\"d\"].max()` → Series indexed by Period with Timestamp values ✓.\n\nmypy: `set(last_per_month)` — iterating a Series over values ✓ set[Timestamp].\n\n`pd.DataFrame.from_dict(rows, orient=\"index\")` — mypy: from_dict accepts dict[str|...]. Dict key type Timestamp — from_dict signature: `from_dict(mapping: Mapping[str, Any], orient...)` — passing a Mapping[Timestamp, Series] → incompatible with Mapping[str, ...]? Mapping is invariant in its key parameter: Mapping[Timestamp, Series] is not a subtype of Mapping[str, Any] → mypy error! Hmm. In pandas 3, from_dict might accept Mapping[Any, Any]? The stub is `def from_dict(cls, data: dict[str, Any], ...)`. Risk of a mypy error.\n\nSafer: build the DataFrame from a dict of columns:\n```python\n        body = {t: {d: float(rows[d][t]) for d in rows} for t in tickers}\n```\nUgh. Alternative:\n```python\n        weights = pd.DataFrame(\n            {t: [float(rows[d].loc[t]) for d in rows] for t in tickers},\n            index=list(rows),\n        )\n```\nrows preserves insertion order = time-sorted ✓ (I break on horizon, so idx is sorted). This is clean, mypy-safe. float(rows[d].loc[t]) — .loc is fine.\n\nActually simpler: since all rows share the same ticker index, I could accumulate a list of numpy arrays… I'll keep the dict-comprehension approach; it's clear.\n\n`weights` dtypes: f