s([\"X\"]))`.\n3. The docstring got broken mid-word by \"AA ... E E\n    EE\" — I wrote this weirdly. Let me fix the docstring.\n4. `build_features(synthetic_prices, store=None, ...)` — store is typed as `object`, and it casts LakeStore to do the write. Passing None will fail at runtime: `cast(\"LakeStore\", store)` → None.write_upsert → AttributeError. So for testing the strategy, I must not call build_features with store=None!\n\nRight — the strategy only needs the feature frame, and build_features writes to the store. Either use the `lake` fixture for the store, or compute the features directly by calling rolling_returns + cross_sectional_scores without going through build_features. A cleaner approach: use the `lake` fixture. This is the existing pipeline path, and tests can use a tmp_path LakeStore.\n\nActually, more simply: in the tests, compute `feats` via the same pure functions that build_features uses: `cross_sectional_scores(rolling_returns(panel, months=(3,6,12), skip_months=cfg.skip_months))` pivoted to wide format. But in that case I'd be re-deriving the plumbing. Using the `lake` fixture is more faithful to the \"pipeline\" and reuses the Task 7 behavior. I'll go with the `lake` fixture.\n\nWait — does build_features with store work? Task 7 has a track record of passing with the lake fixture. Yes, Task 7's tests use it.\n\nAlso the \"rebalance only on scheduled dates\" test: `pos.weights.index` — PositionSet dates: should the weights index be a DatetimeIndex (dates as Timestamps) or plain date objects? Design decision: features.index is a DatetimeIndex, so the position weights' index should also be a DatetimeIndex derived from features.index. PositionSet.dates: list[date] — plain date objects. This matches the interface (dates: list[date]).\n\nThe test checks `day.is_month_end` — for DatetimeIndex entries that works. If I store plain `date` objects in the weights index, `.is_month_end` fails. Keep the weights index as a DatetimeIndex (sorted) — the eng