e: 1.09945 * (1-0.001) = 1.09945 * 0.999 = 1.098350...5. Then * 1.1 = 1.20818555...\n- Day 04: turnover = 1.0. cost_fraction = 1e-3. eq = 1.20818555... * 0.999 = 1.206977365.... * 1.1 = 1.3276751015...\n- Day 05: turnover = 0. cost_fraction = 0. eq = 1.3276751015... * 1 * 1.1 = 1.46044261165...\n\nSo terminal_value (10bp) ≈ 1.4604426 < 1.4641. Good, strictly lower.\n\nHand-check: cost_fraction product = 0.9995 * 0.999 * 0.999 * 1 = 0.9995 * 0.998001 = 0.99750... \nterminal_10bp = terminal_0bp * cost_fraction_product = 1.4641 * 0.997501...? Let me check: 1.4641 * 0.99750 = 1.4604...  Yes matches.\n\nActually let me re-derive: \nterminal_0bp = 1.4641 (the product of (1+held) for days 02-05)\nterminal_10bp = ∏ [ (1 - cost_frac_d) * (1 + held_d) ]\n             = (∏ (1+held_d)) * (∏ (1 - cost_frac_d))\n             = terminal_0bp * (0.9995 * 0.999 * 0.999 * 1)\n\nThe (1 - cost_frac_d) factors are INDEPENDENT of held_d. So:\n\nterminal_10bp = terminal_0bp * 0.9995 * 0.999 * 0.999 * 1\n\nLet me verify:\n0.9995 * 0.999 = 0.9985005\n0.9985005 * 0.999 = 0.9975020395\nSo terminal_10bp = 1.4641 * 0.9975020395 = 1.46044286...\n\nAnd my stepwise calc gives 1.46044261165. Slight difference due to rounding. Both should agree within 1e-9.\n\nActually 1.4641 * 0.9975020395 = 1.460442869... Let me recompute:\n1.4641 * 0.9975020395\n= 1.4641 * (1 - 0.0024979605)\n= 1.4641 - 1.4641 * 0.0024979605\n= 1.4641 - 0.003657266...\n= 1.4604427...\n\nMy stepwise calc: 1.46044261165.\nLet me redo stepwise:\nDay 02: 1.0 * (1-0.0005) * (1+0.1) = 0.9995 * 1.1 = 1.09945\nDay 03: 1.09945 * (1-0.001) * (1+0.1) = 1.09945 * 0.999 * 1.1 = 1.09945 * 1.0989 = 1.208147...\n  1.09945 * 1.0989 = ?\n  1.09945 * 1 = 1.09945\n  1.09945 * 0.0989 = 0.10873...\n  Sum ≈ 1.20818...\nDay 04: 1.20818... * (1-0.001) * 1.1 = 1.20818... * 1.0989 = 1.32754...\nDay 05: 1.32754... * 1 * 1.1 = 1.46029...\n\nHmm, that doesn't quite match. Let me be more careful.\n\n0.999 * 1.1 = 1.0989 (exactly)\nSo each day after day 02: equity *= 1.0989 (for the 10bp days) OR equity *= 1.1 (for the 0b day).\n\nDay 02: 1.0 * 0.9995 * 1.1 = 0.9995 * 1.1 = 1.09945\nDay 03: 1.09945 * 1.0989 = ?\n  1.09945 * 1.0989\n  = 1.09945 * (1 + 0.0989)\n  = 1.09945 + 1.09945 * 0.0989\n  = 1.09945 + 0.108735...\n  = 1.208185...\nDay 04: 1.208185... * 1.0989\n  = 1.208185 * (1 + 0.0989)\n  = 1.208185 + 1.208185 * 0.0989\n  = 1.208185 + 0.119489...\n  = 1.327674...\nDay 05: 1.327674 * 1 * 1.1 = 1.460441...\n\nSo terminal_10bp ≈ 1.460441.\nHand formula: 1.4641 * 0.9975020395 = ?\n  1.4641 * 0.9975020395\n  = 1.4641 * (1 - 0.0024979605)\n  = 1.4641 - 1.4641 * 0.0024979605\n  = 1.4641 - 0.0036573...\n  = 1.4604427...\n\nSo the stepwise and the formula agree: ≈ 1.46044.\n\nThe DoD says \"within 1e-9\" for the 0bp invariant, and \"strictly lower\" for the cost reduction. Both should hold.\n\nNow for the \"cost delta matches turnover-weighted model\" test: the delta = terminal_0bp - terminal_10bp should equal terminal_0bp * (1 - ∏(1 - turnover_d * bps_total)). Actually this is an identity by construction, so the test just verifies the model was implemented correctly. Let me just assert terminal_10bp == terminal_0bp * ∏(1 - turnover_d * bps_total) within 1e-9.\n\nOK, I think I have a clear plan. Let me also think about: what if the position has only 1 day, or the weights are already at their steady-state on day 1? E.g., day 02 w=(1,0), day 03 w=(1,0), day 04 w=(1,0), day 05 w=(1,0). Then turn over on day 02 (buy T), and days 03-05 are no-ops. That's a valid scenario.\n\nLet me also think about the exposure invariant. For every position day, exposure = w_new.sum(). For fully-invested positions this is 1.0. For the first day (first trade), w_new.sum() = 1.0 (AAA=1.0, BBB=0.0). For all other days, w_new.sum() = 1.0. So exposure is 1.0 on every day.\n\nWait, actually in my test scenario, the first trade is day 02 (2024-01-02), not day 01. So positions.dates = [02, 03, 04, 05], and on every one of those days, exposure = 1.0.\n\nBut