omputed ranks for the specific date (AAA highest).\n   - Deterministic: two calls are equal.\n   The rolling_returns implementation must be month-based with calendar searchsorted (not row shift) — the difference from the plan's pseudocode, log as a Ruling (pseudocode's shift is in row units, contradicts the \"months\" semantics; the spec binds: lookback in months).\n\n   Wait, actually — maybe simpler and more standard: ret_{m,m} = px[t - 1 month equivalent] / px[t - (m+1) month equivalent] - 1, \"1 month\" = 21 business days (approximate)? Many momentum implementations use 21 days ≈ 1 month. But the hand-computable test prefers a calendar-month rule using actual dates. With a bdate_range index and date arithmetic (dateutil relativedelta or pandas DateOffset(\"M\")), the reference is exact. I'll implement with DateOffset and searchsorted. Deterministic ✓.\n\n   - T8: PositionSet is already defined (frozen dataclass, dates list[date]). select_top_n: nlargest(n) — tie-break: nlargest preserves order... the test asserts top-2 == ['AAA','BBB'] — with engineered trends, AAA/BBB is clearly top (with 1% vol, the rank is safe — AAA +0.4%/day vs BBB +0.3%/day: over 12 months ≈ 250 days: e^(1.0) ... the difference is 0.1%^250 ≈ e^0.25 vs e^0 → 28% separation over the year... vol 1% daily over 250 days → sigma ≈ 15.8%; the score difference 28%/15.8% ≈ 1.8 sigma — could occasionally flip?? Hmm!! The momentum score is a rank blend of ret3/6/12 — a 12-month rolling return of AAA vs BBB: mean diff 0.1%/day*252 = 25.2%/year, std of the relative diff = sqrt(252)*sqrt(2*0.01^2 - 0) ≈ 0.158*sqrt(2)*... let me compute: std of each ~15.8%; std of the diff ≈ sqrt(2)*15.8% ≈ 22% (assuming independent noise). Mean diff ≈ 28% (e^0.252-1). 1.3 sigma → ~10% flip probability for a single window, across 10 years of monthly rebalances... the test that asserts AAA is top on every schedule date could be flaky!\n\n   Wait — but the fixture is seeded (seed=13), deterministic → a flip is either deter