}", "tool_call_uid": null}, {"id": 125, "session_id": "20261005_100616_a34994", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"success\": true, \"name\": \"superpowers:writing-plans\", \"content\": \"[Bundle context: This skill is part of the 'superpowers' plugin.\\nSibling skills: brainstorming, diagnosing-superpowers, dispatching-parallel-agents, executing-plans, finishing-a-development-branch, receiving-code-review, requesting-code-review, subagent-driven-development, systematic-debugging, test-driven-development, using-git-worktrees, using-superpowers, verification-before-completion, writing-skills.\\nUse qualified form to invoke siblings (e.g. superpowers:brainstorming).]\\n\\n---\\nname: writing-plans\\ndescription: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\\n---\\n\\n# Writing Plans\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nWrite implementation plans for an engineer who has not seen this codebase or this spec. Assume they write idiomatic code in the project's language once they know the exact interface and the exact test, and that they will make a reasonable choice wherever the plan leaves one open. What they cannot know is what you decided: which files, which names and signatures, which values from the spec, which tests prove each task. Document those. Give them the whole plan as bite-sized tasks. DRY. YAGNI. TDD. Frequent commits.\\n\\n**Announce at start:** \\\"I'm using the writing-plans skill to create the implementation plan.\\\"\\n\\n**Context:** If working in an isolated worktree, it should have been created via the `superpowers:using-git-worktrees` skill at execution time.\\n\\n**Save plans to:** `docs/superpowers/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<feature-name>.md`\\n- (User preferences for plan location override this default)\\n\\n## Scope Check\\n\\nIf the spec covers multiple independent subsystems, it should have been broken into sub-project specs during brainstorming. If it wasn't, suggest breaking this into separate plans — one per subsystem. Each plan should produce working, testable software on its own.\\n\\n## File Structure\\n\\nBefore defining tasks, map out which files will be created or modified and what each one is responsible for. This is where decomposition decisions get locked in.\\n\\n- Design units with clear boundaries and well-defined interfaces. Each file should have one clear responsibility.\\n- You reason best about code you can hold in context at once, and your edits are more reliable when files are focused. Prefer smaller, focused files over large ones that do too much.\\n- Files that change together should live together. Split by responsibility, not by technical layer.\\n- In existing codebases, follow established patterns. If the codebase uses large files, don't unilaterally restructure - but if a file you're modifying has grown unwieldy, including a split in the plan is reasonable.\\n\\nThis structure informs the task decomposition. Each task should produce self-contained changes that make sense independently.\\n\\n## Task Right-Sizing\\n\\nA task is the smallest unit that carries its own test cycle and is worth a\\nfresh reviewer's gate. When drawing task boundaries: fold setup,\\nconfiguration, scaffolding, and documentation steps into the task whose\\ndeliverable needs them; split only where a reviewer could meaningfully\\nreject one task while approving its neighbor. Each task ends with an\\nindependently testable deliverable.\\n\\n## Step Granularity\\n\\n**Each step is one action with a checkable result:**\\n- \\\"Write the failing test\\\" - step\\n- \\\"Run it to make sure it fails\\\" - step\\n- \\\"Implement the minimal code to make the test pass\\\" - step\\n- \\\"Run the tests and make sure they pass\\\" - step\\n- \\\"Commit\\\" - step\\n\\n## Plan Document Header\\n\\n**Every plan MUST start with this header:**\\n\\n```markdown\\n# [Feature Name] Implementation Plan\\n\\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\\n\\n**Goal:** [One sentence describing what this builds]\\n\\n**Architecture:** [2-3 sentences about approach]\\n\\n**Tech Stack:** [Key technologies/libraries]\\n\\n**Spec:** [path to the spec/design doc this plan implements — the plan\\nargues from the spec, so the spec travels with it; executors read both]\\n\\n## Global Constraints\\n\\n[The spec's project-wide requirements — version floors, dependency limits,\\nnaming and copy rules, platform requirements — one line each, with exact\\nvalues copied verbatim from the spec. Every task's requirements implicitly\\ninclude this section.]\\n\\n## Review Focus\\n\\n[The five input classes or failure modes the spec implies but no task's\\ntests exercise that are most likely to bite a person using this software\\n— one line each, naming the input or condition and the behavior a\\nreasonable person would expect, most likely first. The spec is a vision\\ndocument: it says what the software must do, not everything it will\\nmeet, and its silence on an input is not permission for that input to\\nbreak the program. Write the list here, once, with the spec in front of\\nyou. Then, for each line, add the test that pins it to the task that\\nowns the code, in that task's own step style.]\\n\\n---\\n```\\n\\n## Task Structure\\n\\n````markdown\\n### Task N: [Component Name]\\n\\n**Files:**\\n- Create: `exact/path/to/file.py`\\n- Modify: `exact/path/to/existing.py:123-145`\\n- Test: `tests/exact/path/to/test.py`\\n\\n**Interfaces:**\\n- Consumes: [what this task uses from earlier tasks — exact signatures]\\n- Produces: [what later tasks rely on — exact function names, parameter\\n  and return types. A task's implementer sees only their own task; this\\n  block is how they learn the names and types neighboring tasks use.]\\n\\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**\\n\\n```python\\ndef test_specific_behavior():\\n    result = function(input)\\n    assert result == expected\\n```\\n\\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run test to verify it fails**\\n\\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\\nExpected: FAIL with \\\"function not defined\\\"\\n\\n- [ ] **Step 3: Implement `function(input: InputType) -> ResultType` in `exact/path/to/file.py`**\\n\\nOne line on the approach when the signature and the test leave a choice\\n(which library call, which data structure); a code block only for an\\nalgorithm they do not determine.\\n\\n- [ ] **Step 4: Run test to verify it passes**\\n\\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\\nExpected: PASS\\n\\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\\n\\n```bash\\ngit add tests/path/test.py src/path/file.py\\ngit commit -m \\\"feat: add specific feature\\\"\\n```\\n````\\n\\n## What a Step Contains\\n\\nA step is done when the implementer can write exactly one reasonable thing\\nfrom it. That is the whole requirement: unambiguous, not complete. Each kind\\nof step carries what makes it unambiguous and nothing more:\\n\\n- **A test step:** the test's name and its assertions, as code, with the\\n  spec's exact values in them.\\n- **A code step:** the exact signature (name, parameters, return type), the\\n  file it lives in, and the specific values the spec pins. The implementer\\n  writes the body. A body appears only for an algorithm the signature and\\n  tests do not determine, or for exact copy the spec fixes.\\n- **A verification step:** the command to run and the output that means it\\n  passed.\\n- **A reference to another task:** that task's Interfaces block says what\\n  to use; the plan does not repeat that task's code.\\n\\nA plan is the set of decisions the implementer cannot make alone. A plan\\nlonger than the code it describes has written the code instead. Lines that\\ndecide nothing (\\\"TBD\\\", \\\"handle edge cases\\\", \\\"add appropriate validation\\\",\\n\\\"write tests for the above\\\", a type or function no task defines) are the\\nopposite failure, and the self-review catches both.\\n\\n## Self-Review\\n\\nAfter writing the complete plan, look at the spec with fresh eyes and check the plan against it. This is a checklist you run yourself — not a subagent dispatch.\\n\\n**1. Spec coverage:** Skim each section/requirement in the spec. Can you point to a task that implements it? List any gaps.\\n\\n**2. Step scan:** Every step must let the implementer write exactly one reasonable thing, and no step may carry more than that: a line that decides nothing is a gap, a function body the signature and tests already determine is a transcript. Fix both.\\n\\n**3. Type consistency:** Do the types, method signatures, and property names you used in later tasks match what you defined in earlier tasks? A function called `clearLayers()` in Task 3 but `clearFullLayers()` in Task 7 is a bug.\\n\\n**4. Review Focus:** For each input class or failure mode the spec implies, is there a task whose tests exercise it? The five uncovered ones most likely to bite a person go in the Review Focus section, and each line there gets its test added to the owning task. An empty section means you checked and found none, not that you skipped the check.\\n\\n**5. Proportion:** Compare the plan's length to the spec's. A plan several times longer than the spec it implements is a transcript of the program, not a plan. If code blocks are most of the document, replace bodies with signatures, test names and assertions, and check that each step is still unambiguous.\\n\\nIf you find issues, fix them inline. No need to re-review — just fix and move on. If you find a spec requirement with no task, add the task.\\n\\n## Execution Handoff\\n\\nAfter saving and self-reviewing the plan, link it for your human partner\\nto read. If they have already explicitly supplied an execution method, ask\\nthem to review the plan and confirm it captures what they want; wait for that\\nreview before implementation, then use the preserved method. Otherwise, ask\\nthem to review the plan and choose an execution method before implementation.\\n\\n**When no execution method has already been supplied:**\\n\\n**\\\"Plan complete and saved to `docs/superpowers/plans/<filename>.md`. Please review the plan. Which execution approach would you prefer?**\\n\\n- **Subagent-driven** - A fresh subagent implements each task and a fresh reviewer checks it before the next one starts, then a whole-branch review at the end. Most thorough; costs a fresh context per task and per review.\\n- **Native** - I implement every task myself in this session, the way this harness runs work, then one fresh reviewer on the most capable model checks the whole branch. Cheapest and fastest; no independent review until the end. Runs well with a mid-tier session model, since the plan carries the design.\\n\\n**For this plan I recommend <one of the two>, because <one sentence from the plan: how much the tasks depend on each other's interfaces, how many there are, what a shipped mistake would cost>. Does the plan capture what you want, and which approach should we use?\\\"**\\n\\n**When an execution method has already been supplied:**\\n\\n**\\\"Plan complete and saved to `docs/superpowers/plans/<filename>.md`. Please review the plan. Does it capture what you want?\\\"**\\n\\n**If Subagent-driven chosen:**\\n- **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL:** Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development\\n\\n**If Native chosen:**\\n- **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL:** Use superpowers:executing-plans\\n\", \"description\": \"Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\", \"linked_files\": null, \"readiness_status\": \"available\", \"skill_dir\": \"/home/ttran/.hermes/plugins/superpowers/skills/writing-plans\", \"_source_path\": \"/home/ttran/.hermes/plugins/superpowers/skills/writing-plans/SKILL.md\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_89pmnvga", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "skill_view", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1791212939.4871988, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0, "api_content": null, "display_kind": null, "display_metadata": null, "message_uid": "d657d999dae142708c98b43946d98f70", "absorbed_message_uids": null, "tool_call_uids": null, "tool_call_uid": "2e1fb968a5f14389a994766ce757e354"}, {"id": 126, "session_id": "20261005_100616_a34994", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1|# Design: S&P 500 Cross-Sectional Momentum System\\n2|\\n3|- **Date:** 2026-10-02\\n4|- **Status:** Proposed\\n5|- **Author:** ttran (with Hermes brainstorming session)\\n6|\\n7|## 1. Purpose\\n8|\\n9|A portfolio-grade demonstration project that proves the ability to build:\\n10|\\n11|1. A real **data pipeline** — scheduled, orchestrated, idempotent — ingesting\\n12|   Yahoo Finance market data for the S&P 500 universe into a queryable store.\\n13|2. A real **trading system** — a clearly defined cross-sectional momentum\\n14|   strategy evaluated by a **custom-built backtesting engine** with explicit\\n15|   cost/slippage modeling and honest performance reporting.\\n16|\\n17|This is a research/proof-of-capability project, not a production trading\\n18|system. It will not place, simulate placement of, or connect to real orders.\\n19|\\n20|## 2. Goals / Non-Goals\\n21|\\n22|**Goals**\\n23|\\n24|- End-to-end pipeline: universe definition → ingest → clean → features →\\n25|  signal → backtest → report, orchestrated as a genuine Airflow DAG.\\n26|- One strategy executed correctly and defensibly: monthly cross-sectional\\n27|  momentum rotation on the S&P 500 (hold top-N by trailing return).\\n28|- Custom backtester (hand-written on pandas/numpy): portfolio construction,\\n29|  rebalancing, transaction cost + slippage model, and standard metrics\\n30|  (CAGR, Sharpe, Sortino, max drawdown, turnover, buy-and-hold benchmark).\\n31|- Deterministic, network-free test suite; hand-verifiable backtest math.\\n32|- Streamlit dashboard showing equity curve, drawdown, holdings over time,\\n33|  and rolling risk metrics.\\n34|- Professional repo hygiene: uv, ruff, mypy, pytest, pre-commit, CI.\\n35|\\n36|**Non-Goals (explicitly out of scope)**\\n37|\\n38|- Live or paper trading, broker integrations.\\n39|- Additional strategies, ML factor mining, multi-asset classes.\\n40|- Sub-daily (intraday) data — daily bars only.\\n41|- Subscriptions, multi-tenancy, production infra hardening.\\n42|\\n43|## 3. Strategic Decisions (agreed in brainstorming)\\n44|\\n45|| Decision | Choice | Rationale |\\n46||---|---|---|\\n47|| Depth | Portfolio demo — breadth and polish, one clean strategy | Strongest per-hour \\\"I can build a system\\\" signal |\\n48|| Strategy | Cross-sectional momentum, top-N monthly rotation | Natural fit for a 500-ticker pipeline; honest, defensible math |\\n49|| Strategy library | **Custom lightweight backtester** written by hand | Defensible core of the project; not just configuring a library |\\n50|| Universe | S&P 500 constituents (via Wikipedia list, date-stamped) | Strongest \\\"real system\\\" signal; moderate data volume |\\n51|| Data store | DuckDB over Parquet files (lake layout) | Zero-infra database semantics; honest pipeline story |\\n52|| **Orchestrator** | **Apache Airflow** (local dev; Docker Compose for server+worker) | Chosen for strongest data-engineering signal; tasks kept framework-agnostic so the DAG wrapper stays thin |\\n53|| UI | Streamlit | Fastest credible trading dashboard |\\n54|| Python | 3.12 pinned (`.python-version`) | Broadest compatibility for the stack; system 3.14 is too new for some wheels |\\n55|\\n56|## 4. Architecture\\n57|\\n58|### 4.1 High-level data flow\\n59|\\n60|```\\n61|universe (spx500.csv, date-stamped)\\n62|   │\\n63|   ▼\\n64|[ingest] yfinance daily OHLCV → raw/          (Airflow task)\\n65|   │\\n66|   ▼\\n67|[transform] dedupe, gap check, adjust prices, align calendars → clean/\\n68|   │\\n69|   ▼\\n70|[features] trailing 3/6/12-mo returns, rank scores → features/\\n71|   │\\n72|   ▼\\n73|[signal] monthly rebalance: rank → top-N → target weights → positions/\\n74|   │\\n75|   ▼\\n76|[backtest] custom engine + cost model → results (equity curve, trades)\\n77|   │\\n78|   ▼\\n79|[report + dashboard] metrics summary, plots, Streamlit app\\n80|```\\n81|\\n82|Every bracketed stage is one **task function** with explicit typed I/O,\\n83|wired into an Airflow DAG. Task functions live in\\n84|`src/spx_momentum/pipeline/tasks.py` and are pure Python (no Airflow\\n85|imports), so they are directly unit-testable and portable.\\n86|\\n87|### 4.2 Components\\n88|\\n89|**Config (`src/spx_momentum/config.py`)**\\n90|Pydantic settings loaded from `config/default.yaml` (overridable by env).\\n91|Holds: universe source, N (default 20), lookback months (default 12,\\n92|skipping the most recent 1 month — classic 12-1 momentum), rebalance\\n93|schedule (last trading day of month), transaction cost bps, slippage bps,\\n94|data paths.\\n95|\\n96|**Data layer (`src/spx_momentum/data/`)**\\n97|- `universe.py` — fetch S&P 500 constituents (Wikipedia), validate\\n98|  (tickers well-formed, count in [480, 530]), snapshot with fetch date.\\n99|- `ingest.py` — yfinance download with chunking, per-ticker retry (3x,\\n100|  exponential backoff), partial-failure tolerance, writes raw Parquet.\\n101|  Records fetch metadata (timestamp, rows, null counts) per ticker.\\n102|- `transform.py` — validation (null ratios, date coverage, bad bars),\\n103|  use adjusted close, align all tickers to a common trading calendar,\\n104|  upsert into clean Parquet keyed by (ticker, date) — idempotent.\\n105|- `store.py` — thin DuckDB/Parquet read/write helpers shared by all stages.\\n106|\\n107|**Pipeline (`src/spx_momentum/pipeline/`)**\\n108|- `tasks.py` — task functions (universe, ingest, transform, features,\\n109|  signal, backtest, report). Each takes and returns explicit data paths\\n110|  (file-based passing — no shared in-memory state between tasks, so tasks\\n111|  are independently rerunnable and inspectable).\\n112|- The Airflow DAG lives in the top-level `dags/` folder (Airflow's\\n113|  conventional DAG location) and only imports the package's task functions:\\n114|  `dags/momentum_dag.py` — Airflow DAG: schedule, task dependencies,\\n115|  retries (2) with backoff on network stages, `execution_timeout` /\\n116|  `retry_delay`, tags. Thin: it only wires task functions.\\n117|- Compose: `docker compose up` → Airflow webserver + scheduler + worker\\n118|  (or local in-process scheduler for simplest dev: `airflow standalone`).\\n119|\\n120|**Features (`src/spx_momentum/features/momentum.py`)**\\n121|Compute rolling returns (3/6/12 months, 1-month skip), rank cross-section,\\n122|emit per-date score tables. Pure pandas; deterministic.\\n123|\\n124|**Strategy (`src/spx_momentum/strategy/`)**\\n125|- `base.py` — `Strategy` ABC: `generate_target_weights(prices, features, as_of) -> PositionSet`.\\n126|- `signal.py` — top-N selection, equal weights, rebalance only on schedule\\n127|  dates, produces (date, ticker, weight) tables.\\n128|\\n129|**Backtest (`src/spx_momentum/backtest/`)**\\n130|- `engine.py` — custom engine: on each rebalance date, compute turnover\\n131|  vs previous weights, apply cost model to the traded notional, hold\\n132|  weights, mark-to-market daily between rebalances. Returns: equity curve,\\n133|  trade log, exposure.\\n134|- `costs.py` — proportional cost bps + slippage bps, applied one-way on\\n135|  turnover; configurable.\\n136|- `metrics.py` — CAGR, annualized vol, Sharpe, Sortino, max drawdown\\n137|  (and date), Calmar, turnover, win rate on rebalance periods,\\n138|  vs equal-weight S&P 500 (SPY) benchmark.\\n139|- `report.py` — writes human-readable `artifacts/report.md` + equity\\n140|  curve + drawdown + top-10 holdings-heatmap (matplotlib, no JS deps).\\n141|\\n142|**UI (`src/spx_momentum/ui/dashboard.py`)**\\n143|Streamlit app reading artifacts + Parquet: equity curve vs benchmark\\n144|(toggle), drawdown band, metrics table, holdings-over-time selection,\\n145|parameter re-run (N, lookback, costs) that executes the backtest in-process\\n146|for a \\\"what-if\\\" mode.\\n147|\\n148|### 4.3 Repo layout\\n149|\\n150|```\\n151|spx-momentum/\\n152|  pyproject.toml            # uv-managed; ruff/mypy/pytest config inline\\n153|  .python-version           # 3.12\\n154|  Makefile                  # sync, test, lint, run-dag, dashboard\\n155|  Dockerfile (app), docker-compose.yaml  # airflow standalone profile\\n156|  dags/momentum_dag.py      # thin DAG importing package tasks\\n157|  config/default.yaml\\n158|  data/                     # gitignored lake: raw/, clean/, features/, positions/, results/\\n159|  src/spx_momentum/         # package per §4.2\\n160|  tests/                    # unit + one end-to-end smoke (synthetic data)\\n161|  docs/superpowers/specs/   # this design doc\\n162|  docs/methodology.md       # how it works, biases (survivorship, lookback), caveats\\n163|  artifacts/                # gitignored outputs\\n164|  .github/workflows/ci.yml  # lint + test\\n165|  .gitignore, README.md\\n166|```\\n167|\\n168|### 4.4 Error handling & idempotency\\n169|\\n170|- Every stage writes to its own lake directory keyed by (ticker, date)\\n171|  with upsert semantics → safe reruns, no duplicate accumulation.\\n172|- Network stages: retries with exponential backoff; per-ticker isolation\\n173|  (one dead ticker never fails the DAG; failures logged to a fetch-manifest\\n174|  table the pipeline reports on).\\n175|- Validation gates: transform refuses to emit if a ticker's null-ratio or\\n176|  date coverage exceeds thresholds (configurable); a failed gate fails the\\n177|  task loudly rather than silently propagating bad data.\\n178|- Backtest/report are pure functions of stored tables → rerunnable\\n179|  deterministically without network.\\n180|\\n181|### 4.5 Testing strategy\\n182|\\n183|- **No network in tests.** yfinance is monkeypatched; the universe CSV is\\n184|  a fixture. A deterministic synthetic price generator (seeded, 3-5\\n185|  tickers, multi-year, with engineered trends) drives feature/signal/\\n186|  backtest tests so results are hand-verifiable.\\n187|- **Key invariants asserted:**\\n188|  - Top-N selection picks the actual top performers on synthetic data.\\n189|  - Rebalance occurs only on schedule dates; weights sum to 1 before costs.\\n190|  - A pure no-cost run's equity curve equals the hand-computed geometric\\n191|    product of held returns (exact match within float tol).\\n192|  - Adding 10 bp cost strictly reduces terminal value; the delta matches\\n193|    the turnover-weighted cost model.\\n194|  - Sharpe/Sortino/max-drawdown match closed-form hand calculations on a\\n195|    6-observation toy series.\\n196|- **End-to-end smoke:** full task-function chain (no Airflow) on the\\n197|  synthetic universe; asserts every lake stage produces consistent row\\n198|  counts.\\n199|- DAG wiring itself is smoke-checked by importing and asserting task\\n200|  dependencies (Airflow programmatic-API test).\\n201|\\n202|## 5. Risks & mitigations\\n203|\\n204|| Risk | Mitigation |\\n205||---|---|\\n206|| yfinance rate limiting on 503 tickers | Chunked downloads (≈25/ticker calls or bulk download), per-ticker retry + backoff, caching raw data so reruns don't refetch |\\n207|| Wikipedia universe scrape format changes | Pinned parsing with strict validation; snapshot CSV committed into git as the fallback |\\n208|| Survivorship bias (constituence list is today's) | Documented explicitly in methodology.md as a known limitation; noted in report |\\n209|| Python 3.14 wheel gaps (system) | Pin 3.12 via uv/`.python-version`; no reliance on system Python deps |\\n210|| Airflow local friction (env vars, alembic) | Provide `Makefile` one-command `airflow standalone` profile; Docker Compose as alternative |\\n211|| Over-polish / scope creep | Non-goals above are hard limits; YAGNI enforced in plan |\\n212|\\n213|## 6. Skills being built (learning outcomes)\\n214|\\n215|1. Data-pipeline & orchestration (tasks, DAGs, retries, idempotency) — Airflow\\n216|2. Analytical storage: Parquet lake + DuckDB SQL from Python\\n217|3. Custom quantitative backtesting math: portfolio construction, cost\\n218|   modeling, Sharpe/Sortino/drawdown from first principles\\n219|4. Cross-sectional momentum strategy design & 12-1 lookback conventions\\n220|5. Deterministic testing of data/quant code with synthetic fixtures\\n221|6. Python packaging/professional tooling: uv, ruff, mypy, pre-commit, CI\\n222|7. Streamlit dashboarding of quantitative results\\n223|8. yfinance data quirks: adjusted prices, rate limits, missing bars\\n224|\\n225|## 7. Effort estimate\\n226|\\n227|| Phase | Work | Hours |\\n228||---|---|---|\\n229|| 0 | Repo, uv, ruff, mypy, pytest, pre-commit, CI | 4–8 |\\n230|| 1 | Data pipeline (universe→ingest→transform→store) + Airflow wiring | 18–32 |\\n231|| 2 | Momentum features + strategy signal + rebalance logic | 8–16 |\\n232|| 3 | Custom backtest engine + cost model + metrics (core) | 24–40 |\\n233|| 4 | Report + Streamlit dashboard + methodology doc | 16–24 |\\n234|| 5 | Polish: README, plots, E2E run, demo script | 8–16 |\\n235|| | **Total** | **≈ 100–135 h** |\\n236|\\n237|Rhythm options: part-time ~10 h/week → 2.5–3.5 months; full-time → 3–5 weeks.\\n238|\\n239|Milestones (in order, each independently demonstrable):\\n240|1. Single ticker ingested → validated → queryable in DuckDB.\\n241|2. Full universe pipeline green + DAG run clean on Airflow.\\n242|3. First backtest with cost model + hand-verified metrics.\\n243|4. Dashboard tells the full story with a \\\"what-if\\\" rerun.\", \"total_lines\": 243, \"file_size\": 12042, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false, \"not_found\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call_493stbxk", "too